{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_1_guesses": "never", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_2_guesses": "never", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "The puzzles were all harder than the warm up one, mostly due to smaller numbers, and having few rows that are immediately locked in (e.g. 8 1 is immediately clear)", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "Similar to the rest, there were very few rows that could be solved just by themselves so it required a lot of going back over the puzzle and checking positions of the numbers and x squares to fill in the tiles. Most hints consist of numbers <4 which meant there were many more possible arrangements that had to be ruled out first.", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "I found it around the same difficulty as the one before it, it only had one or two lines that could be solved by themselves and both were in the middle of the square which made it more difficult fill squares quickly.", "strategy": "Some strategies I used:\n- If there aren't any obvious rows/columns, starting off with the largest numbers/most numbers and checking the two opposing arrangements to see what squares are in common and must be filled (e.g. a 3 4 guarantees the third, seventh and eighth squares are filled)\n- if a row/column on the edge gets completed, its easy to get the first or last number in that row/column based on it", "comments": "The fact that it is a 10x10 inherently made the puzzles pretty simple but I'd say that they're still on the harder side of possible 10x10s"}, "t": "2026-06-04T20:40:25.467062+00:00"}
{"type": "session_end", "t": "2026-06-04T20:27:16.343223+00:00"}
